/**
 * Written introductions to each London borough.
 *
 * A borough page aggregates its neighbourhoods' data — average rents,
 * lifestyle scores, council tax — and that part is shared in shape with
 * the other 31. This is the part that is not: the borough's geography,
 * how different its halves are, what lies beyond the areas tracked here,
 * and what being a resident of this council actually means. Keyed by
 * the borough slug. Hand-written; nothing that goes stale in a year;
 * never contradicting the figures on the page.
 */
export const BOROUGH_EDITORIAL: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "barking-and-dagenham": [
    "Barking and Dagenham stretches along the north bank of the Thames on London's eastern edge, and it has the lowest rents and house prices of any London borough. Its identity was forged by industry and public housing. The Becontree estate, built between the wars, was one of the largest council estates in the world, and Ford's Dagenham works was for decades one of the biggest car plants in Europe, employing tens of thousands of local people.",
    "Barking, in the west, is the borough's main town and transport hub, with the District and Hammersmith & City lines, the Overground and fast c2c trains to Fenchurch Street. Dagenham, further east, is more suburban, served by the District line. Barking Riverside, a large new town on former industrial land beside the Thames, has its own Overground station and river bus pier.",
    "The borough has a young and rapidly changing population, with large African, South Asian and Eastern European communities alongside long-established white working-class families. Green spaces include Barking Park, Mayesbrook Park, Parsloes Park and Eastbrookend Country Park, and the Thames riverside is being opened up as development continues.",
    "For renters, the appeal is space and value: houses with gardens and flats at rents far below inner London, with journey times to the City that can be surprisingly short from Barking itself. The trade-offs are distance from central and west London, fewer amenities and nightlife, and neighbourhoods that are still working through the effects of deindustrialisation. Beyond Barking itself, Dagenham, Becontree and Chadwell Heath are worth a look for anyone whose first priority is the rent: the District line still runs through them, and three-bedroom houses with gardens come up at prices that would barely rent a one-bed flat in Hackney.",
  ],

  barnet: [
    "Barnet is London's most populous borough, a vast area of suburbs stretching from the edge of Hampstead Heath to the Hertfordshire countryside. It includes busy centres such as Finchley, Hendon and Golders Green, quieter suburbs such as Mill Hill, Totteridge and Edgware, and the historic market town of High Barnet at its northern tip. Much of the north of the borough is protected green belt.",
    "Both northern branches of the Northern line end in Barnet, at Edgware and High Barnet, and Thameslink serves Hendon, Mill Hill Broadway and the new Brent Cross West station. That makes the borough well connected to the West End and City along its north–south corridors, but east–west journeys are slower, and the North Circular and M1 carry heavy traffic through the south of the borough.",
    "Barnet has one of the largest Jewish communities in Britain, concentrated in Golders Green, Hendon, Finchley and Edgware, alongside substantial Indian, Chinese, Iranian, Korean and Eastern European communities. It is known for strong schools, including several highly regarded state and grammar schools, which makes it popular with families.",
    "Hampstead Garden Suburb, the early twentieth-century planned suburb of Arts and Crafts houses, lies on the borough's southern edge, and the Brent Cross regeneration is creating a new town centre with homes and offices in the south-west. The borough's green spaces include Hampstead Heath Extension, Dollis Valley Greenwalk, Mill Hill Park and Totteridge's fields. In practice, people choose Barnet for what a Northern line suburb gives a household — a garden, a school place, a quieter street — and accept that a night out usually means a train back into Camden or the West End.",
  ],

  bexley: [
    "Bexley is London's south-eastern corner, bordering Kent, and it is one of the most suburban boroughs in the capital. Its main centres — Bexleyheath, Sidcup, Welling, Erith and Crayford — are largely made up of interwar semi-detached houses with gardens, and car ownership is higher than in most of London.",
    "There is no Underground or DLR in the borough. Southeastern trains run to London Bridge, Cannon Street and Charing Cross along three routes, and the Elizabeth line at Abbey Wood, on the borough's northern edge, has transformed access to the City, Canary Wharf and the West End for residents within reach of it. Journeys from the southern suburbs are longer.",
    "Bexley has more history and green space than many visitors expect. Red House, built for William Morris, is one of the most important Arts and Crafts buildings in the world; Hall Place is a Tudor mansion with formal gardens; Danson Park has a lake and a restored Georgian villa; and Lesnes Abbey Woods, near Abbey Wood, combine medieval abbey ruins with ancient woodland and a fossil bed. The Thames riverside at Erith has one of the longest piers in London.",
    "The borough's grammar schools and relatively low housing costs make it popular with families, and its population is older and more settled than inner London's. Rents are among the lowest in south-east London. The trade-offs are distance from central London, limited nightlife and a strongly car-oriented lifestyle in many areas. Sidcup, Welling and Erith offer similar suburban value to Bexleyheath, and Erith's riverside is changing as new flats replace old industrial land along the water.",
  ],

  brent: [
    "Brent is one of the most diverse places in Britain, with large Indian, Pakistani, Irish, Caribbean, Somali, Brazilian, Polish and Romanian communities, and more than a hundred languages spoken locally. It stretches from Kilburn and Kensal Rise in the south-east to Wembley, Kingsbury and the edge of Harrow in the north-west.",
    "Wembley is its most famous landmark. Wembley Stadium hosts football finals, England internationals and major concerts, and the Wembley Park development around it has added thousands of new homes, a large arena and shops. Event days bring huge crowds and road closures, but also some of the best-connected new-build rental housing in outer north-west London, served by the Jubilee and Metropolitan lines and Chiltern trains.",
    "The borough has other distinctive places. The BAPS Shri Swaminarayan Mandir in Neasden is one of the largest Hindu temples outside India; the Welsh Harp reservoir is a nature reserve and sailing lake; and Kensal Rise and Queen's Park, in the south-east, are sought-after neighbourhoods of Victorian terraces, independent cafés and parks. Harlesden, Willesden and Neasden are more workaday but offer lower rents.",
    "Transport varies widely. The Jubilee line serves Kilburn, Willesden Green and Wembley Park; the Bakerloo and Overground serve Queen's Park, Kensal Green, Harlesden and Stonebridge Park; and the Metropolitan line runs fast trains to Baker Street from Wembley Park. The practical rule in Brent is to pick a transport corridor first and a neighbourhood second: rents fall steeply as you move away from the Jubilee line, and a flat in Harlesden or Stonebridge can cost far less than one a mile east in Queen's Park while still reaching the West End in under half an hour.",
  ],

  bromley: [
    "Bromley is London's largest borough by area, and much of it does not feel like London at all. More than half of the borough is green belt, and its southern parts are rolling countryside, farmland, woods and villages, including Downe, where Charles Darwin lived and worked at Down House. Biggin Hill airport, famous for its role in the Battle of Britain, is also within the borough.",
    "The northern half is suburban and affluent. Bromley town centre is a large shopping destination, and Beckenham, Chislehurst, Orpington, Petts Wood and West Wickham are sought-after suburbs of Edwardian and interwar houses with gardens. Chislehurst has an old village centre, a large common and the Chislehurst Caves, a network of tunnels once used as air raid shelters.",
    "Crystal Palace Park, with its Victorian dinosaur sculptures and sports centre, lies in the borough's north-western corner, where Bromley meets four other boroughs. The Crystal Palace neighbourhood around it is covered in detail here, and it is more urban, creative and lively than most of the borough.",
    "There is no Underground in Bromley. Southeastern and Southern trains serve Bromley South, Beckenham Junction, Orpington and many smaller stations, reaching Victoria, London Bridge, Cannon Street and Charing Cross in around twenty to thirty-five minutes, and the Overground and trams serve the north-west. Many residents drive. Many households arrive in Bromley when a second child makes a flat impossible, and stay for the combination of big gardens, woods within a short drive and a train to Victoria that takes little longer than a tube ride from Zone 3.",
  ],

  camden: [
    "Camden covers an extraordinary range of London in a relatively small area. Its southern edge touches Holborn and Covent Garden; Bloomsbury and King's Cross hold universities, the British Museum and the British Library; Camden Town and Kentish Town are known for music and markets; and Hampstead, Highgate and Belsize Park are among the most expensive residential areas in the country.",
    "That range creates sharp contrasts. The borough has some of the grandest period homes in London alongside large council estates, and its population includes students, academics, creative workers, families who have lived there for generations and very wealthy residents. Rents vary as widely as the housing, from rooms in shared flats near Kentish Town to large apartments beside the Heath.",
    "Green space is one of Camden's great strengths. Hampstead Heath occupies much of the borough's north, Regent's Park and Primrose Hill lie on its western edge, and the Regent's Canal winds through Camden Town towards King's Cross. The borough is also home to major institutions, including University College London, the Francis Crick Institute, the Royal Free Hospital and several leading hospitals.",
    "Transport is excellent. The Northern line runs through the borough's spine, King's Cross St Pancras and Euston are major rail hubs, and the Overground crosses the north of the borough. The long-running construction around Euston for new high-speed rail services has disrupted streets near the station. The borough rewards careful choosing more than most: a room near Kentish Town and a mansion flat in Belsize Park can be twenty minutes' walk apart and several hundred pounds a month different, while sharing the same Heath and the same Northern line.",
  ],

  croydon: [
    "Croydon is one of London's largest boroughs by population, and it contains a big, busy town centre of its own with office towers, shopping, arts venues and one of the fastest rail links to central London. It stretches from the dense, diverse neighbourhoods of Thornton Heath, Norbury and Selhurst in the north to leafy suburbs such as Purley, Coulsdon and Sanderstead in the south, where the borough meets the Surrey countryside.",
    "East Croydon station has fast Thameslink and Southern trains to London Bridge and Victoria, as well as to Gatwick and Brighton, and Croydon's tram network links the town centre to Wimbledon, Beckenham and New Addington. West Croydon has the Overground towards Shoreditch and Highbury. There is no Underground.",
    "The borough's council has faced serious financial problems in recent years, and parts of the town centre, including the main shopping area awaiting redevelopment, have looked neglected. At the same time, office-to-residential conversions, new towers and venues such as Boxpark and the refurbished Fairfield Halls have brought new residents and activity. Croydon is home to Crystal Palace FC's ground at Selhurst Park and to a large Home Office immigration centre.",
    "Green spaces include Lloyd Park, Addington Hills, Wandle Park, Norwood Grove and the downland commons of the south. Council tax is among the highest in London, which is worth factoring into the monthly cost. The honest summary is that Croydon trades polish for speed and space: few places this far out get you to London Bridge as quickly, and few offer such a spread of housing, from a studio in a converted office block to a family house on the slopes above Purley.",
  ],

  ealing: [
    "Ealing is a large west London borough of seven distinct towns: Ealing, Acton, Southall, Hanwell, Greenford, Northolt and Perivale. Together they range from affluent Victorian and Edwardian suburbs to industrial estates, busy multicultural high streets and interwar housing, and the borough has one of the largest populations in London.",
    "The Elizabeth line runs through the borough, stopping at Acton Main Line, Ealing Broadway, West Ealing, Hanwell and Southall, and has made journeys to Paddington, the West End, the City and Heathrow much faster. The Central line serves Ealing Broadway, North Acton, Perivale, Greenford and Northolt, the District and Piccadilly lines serve Acton and Ealing Common, and the Overground runs through Acton.",
    "Southall is home to one of the largest Punjabi communities outside India, and its Broadway is famous for South Asian restaurants, sweet shops, jewellers and textile stores. Hanwell has the Wharncliffe Viaduct, designed by Isambard Kingdom Brunel, and Perivale has the Hoover Building, one of London's best-known art deco landmarks. Ealing Studios has been making films for more than a century.",
    "Green space is plentiful: Walpole Park, Gunnersbury Park, Horsenden Hill, Brent River Park and the Grand Union Canal all run through the borough. Hanwell, West Ealing and Southall sit on the same Elizabeth line corridor as Ealing Broadway and usually cost noticeably less, which makes them some of the better-value stops for anyone commuting to Paddington, the West End or Heathrow. Greenford and Northolt, on the Central line further north, are cheaper still and more suburban.",
  ],

  enfield: [
    "Enfield is London's northernmost borough, bordering Hertfordshire, and it has a clear divide between west and east. The western side, around Enfield Town, Winchmore Hill, Southgate and Grange Park, is leafy and suburban, with large houses, parks and some of the borough's best schools. The eastern side, including Edmonton, Ponders End and Enfield Highway, is more industrial, denser and more affordable, with long-standing working-class communities.",
    "Green space is extensive. Trent Park, Forty Hall and Whitewebbs are large country estates with woodland and farmland, and the Lee Valley Regional Park, with its reservoirs, nature reserves and cycle routes, runs along the eastern boundary. The borough has a large Greek Cypriot and Turkish Cypriot population, particularly in Palmers Green and Edmonton, alongside many other communities.",
    "Transport varies across the borough. The Piccadilly line serves Southgate, Arnos Grove, Oakwood and Cockfosters in the west, with Southgate's circular 1930s station one of the finest designed by Charles Holden. The Overground runs from Enfield Town and Edmonton Green to Liverpool Street, Great Northern trains serve Enfield Chase, Winchmore Hill and Palmers Green, and Greater Anglia trains run through the Lea Valley. Journeys to central London generally take thirty to fifty minutes.",
    "Rents are relatively low, especially in the east of the borough, and the supply of family houses with gardens is far larger than in inner London. The price is time: most journeys to central London take the best part of an hour door to door, so Enfield works best for people who commute a few days a week or work locally.",
  ],

  greenwich: [
    "The Royal Borough of Greenwich stretches along the south bank of the Thames from Deptford Creek to Thamesmead, and it contains some of the most varied places in south-east London. Maritime Greenwich, with the Royal Observatory, National Maritime Museum, Old Royal Naval College and Greenwich Park, is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, while the Greenwich Peninsula has been transformed by the O2 arena and thousands of new homes.",
    "Woolwich, further east, was home to the Royal Arsenal, which manufactured munitions for the armed forces for centuries; its historic buildings have been converted into flats, restaurants and a theatre, and the Elizabeth line now reaches the City in around twenty minutes. Eltham, to the south, is a suburban town with Eltham Palace, a medieval royal palace with an art deco interior. Charlton has Charlton House, a Jacobean mansion, and Charlton Athletic's ground.",
    "The borough has plenty of green space, including Greenwich Park, Blackheath (shared with Lewisham), Shooters Hill, Oxleas Wood, an ancient woodland, and the Thames Path along the river. The cable car across the Thames links the Greenwich Peninsula to the Royal Docks.",
    "Transport includes the Jubilee line at North Greenwich, the DLR through Greenwich and Woolwich, Southeastern rail services across the borough, the Elizabeth line at Woolwich and Abbey Wood, and river buses from several piers. Woolwich, Charlton and the Peninsula offer more new-build housing than historic Greenwich and often lower rents, and Woolwich's Elizabeth line station has turned a once-overlooked town into one of the quicker commutes to the City from south of the river.",
  ],

  hackney: [
    "Hackney has become shorthand for creative east London, but it is a large and complex borough with sharp contrasts. It includes Shoreditch and Hoxton on the City fringe, the markets and parks of London Fields and Hackney Central, the bars of Dalston, the canals of Hackney Wick, the independent shops of Stoke Newington, and the large Orthodox Jewish community of Stamford Hill in the north.",
    "The borough has a large share of social housing and some of the highest levels of deprivation in London, alongside rapidly rising rents and house prices. Its population is young and diverse, with long-established Caribbean, African, Turkish, Kurdish and Vietnamese communities, many of whom have seen their neighbourhoods change dramatically in recent decades.",
    "Hackney is famous for having very few Underground stations, and most residents rely on the Overground, which runs north–south through Dalston and Hoxton and east–west through Hackney Central and Hackney Wick, and also connects Hackney Downs, Clapton, Stoke Newington and Rectory Road to Liverpool Street. Buses are heavily used, and the borough has one of the highest rates of cycling in London.",
    "Green space is a real strength. Victoria Park lies on the borough's southern edge, and Hackney Marshes, the Lee Valley, Clissold Park, London Fields, Hackney Downs, Springfield Park and Abney Park are all within the borough. The Regent's Canal and River Lea provide traffic-free routes for walking and cycling. Living well in Hackney mostly comes down to accepting its transport on its own terms: the Overground, a bike and the occasional long bus ride, in exchange for markets, parks and some of the liveliest streets in the city on your doorstep.",
  ],

  "hammersmith-and-fulham": [
    "Hammersmith and Fulham is a long, narrow borough running from Wormwood Scrubs and White City in the north to the Thames at Fulham in the south. It combines busy transport hubs, riverside neighbourhoods, large regeneration areas and some of west London's most popular streets for young professionals and families.",
    "The borough is home to three professional football clubs — Chelsea at Stamford Bridge, Fulham at Craven Cottage and Queens Park Rangers at Loftus Road — which is unusual even by London standards. It also contains the BBC's former Television Centre, now homes, offices and studios, Imperial College's White City campus, Westfield London and part of the Old Oak Common area being redeveloped around a major new rail interchange.",
    "Hammersmith is the main commercial centre, with four tube lines and the Eventim Apollo, while Shepherd's Bush has Westfield, the Shepherd's Bush Empire and the Bush Theatre. Fulham, in the south, is almost entirely residential, with long streets of Victorian and Edwardian terraces, riverside parks and a strong pub culture. The Thames Path runs along much of the borough's southern and western edges.",
    "Transport is generally good in the north and centre of the borough, with the Central, District, Piccadilly, Hammersmith & City and Circle lines and the Overground, but parts of Fulham rely on the slower District line and buses. Band D council tax here is one of the three lowest in London, a real saving over a year compared with neighbouring Ealing or Hounslow. Match days are the main local disruption: three clubs in one borough means that on some weekends the pubs, pavements and District line fill with fans.",
  ],

  haringey: [
    "Haringey is a borough of two halves, and the difference between them is one of the starkest in London. The western side, including Highgate, Muswell Hill and Crouch End, sits on hills with large Victorian and Edwardian houses, independent high streets and some of north London's most sought-after schools. The eastern side, including Tottenham, Northumberland Park and parts of Wood Green, is flatter, denser, more diverse and more affordable, with large estates and significant deprivation.",
    "The dividing line broadly follows the railway through Harringay and Hornsey, and it is visible in rents, housing, amenities and transport. Harringay's Green Lanes, with its long run of Turkish and Kurdish restaurants and grocers, sits between the two halves and is one of the borough's liveliest streets.",
    "The borough has two major landmarks. Alexandra Palace, on a hilltop in the west, has a park, ice rink, concert halls and panoramic views across London. Tottenham Hotspur's stadium, in the east, has driven regeneration along Tottenham High Road. Green space also includes Finsbury Park, Highgate Wood, Queen's Wood, the Parkland Walk and the Lee Valley, including Tottenham Marshes.",
    "Transport is strong in the east and centre but weaker on the western hills. The Victoria line serves Seven Sisters and Tottenham Hale, the Piccadilly line serves Manor House, Turnpike Lane, Wood Green and Bounds Green, and the Northern line serves Highgate. Crouch End and Muswell Hill have no tube stations and rely on buses and Great Northern trains. The irony of the borough is that its cheaper eastern half usually has the better commute, so the choice most renters face is between the hillside villages and a faster, less expensive journey from Tottenham or Wood Green.",
  ],

  harrow: [
    "Harrow is a north-west London borough of suburbs that grew rapidly along the Metropolitan line in the early twentieth century, when the railway company marketed the area as Metro-land. Today it includes Harrow town centre, the historic hilltop village of Harrow on the Hill, and suburbs such as Pinner, Stanmore, Wealdstone, Rayners Lane, Kenton and Edgware.",
    "It is one of the most religiously and ethnically diverse boroughs in the country, with large Hindu, Muslim, Jewish and Christian communities and a particularly large Gujarati population. Temples, mosques, synagogues and churches sit alongside restaurants and grocers reflecting South Asian, Sri Lankan, Middle Eastern and Eastern European cultures.",
    "Harrow on the Hill is dominated by Harrow School and St Mary's Church, and its steep lanes offer long views across London. Pinner has a village high street with timber-framed buildings and the Heath Robinson Museum, and Stanmore has Bentley Priory, the Battle of Britain headquarters now used as a museum. Green spaces include Harrow Weald Common, Stanmore Common, Canons Park and Headstone Manor Park.",
    "The Metropolitan line runs fast trains from Harrow-on-the-Hill to Baker Street, and the Jubilee line serves Stanmore and Canons Park. The Bakerloo line and Overground reach Harrow & Wealdstone, and Chiltern trains serve Harrow-on-the-Hill and Sudbury Hill. Journeys to central London are faster than distance suggests from the main stations. For many families the calculation is simple: a house with a garden within a short walk of a fast Metropolitan line station, in a borough whose temples, mosques, synagogues and churches reflect one of the most mixed populations in the country.",
  ],

  havering: [
    "Havering is London's easternmost borough, bordering Essex, and it feels more like the Essex suburbs than inner London. Its main town, Romford, is a large shopping and nightlife centre, and its suburbs include Hornchurch, Upminster, Harold Wood, Gidea Park, Collier Row and Rainham. Around half of the borough is green belt.",
    "It is one of the greenest boroughs in London, with Havering Country Park, Bedfords Park, Hornchurch Country Park, Harrow Lodge Park, Upminster Park and the RSPB's Rainham Marshes nature reserve beside the Thames. Upminster has a restored windmill, and Rainham has a Georgian hall owned by the National Trust.",
    "The Elizabeth line serves Romford, Gidea Park and Harold Wood, giving direct trains to Liverpool Street, the West End and beyond. The District line terminates at Upminster, which also has fast c2c trains to Fenchurch Street, and the Overground runs between Romford and Upminster. Journeys from the borough's suburbs to central London are relatively long, and car ownership is high, particularly outside Romford.",
    "Havering's population is older and less diverse than much of London, though it has become more mixed in recent years. Housing is dominated by interwar semi-detached houses, bungalows and family homes with gardens, and rents are among the lowest in London. Its council tax is one of the higher bills in the capital, which narrows the gap with inner boroughs a little. Even so, a household moving here from inner east London usually gains a garden, a driveway and a quieter street for less money each month.",
  ],

  hillingdon: [
    "Hillingdon is London's westernmost borough and its second largest by area, stretching from Northwood and Harefield in the north to Heathrow Airport in the south. Heathrow is by far its largest employer and shapes much of the southern part of the borough, from aircraft noise to hotels, logistics depots and the thousands of residents who work at the airport.",
    "The borough has a strong suburban and rural character. Uxbridge is its main town, home to Brunel University and the Battle of Britain Bunker. Ruislip has a village high street, Ruislip Lido and Ruislip Woods, a national nature reserve. Northwood, Eastcote and Ickenham are leafy suburbs, and Harefield is a village surrounded by fields near the Hertfordshire border.",
    "Green space is abundant. The Colne Valley Regional Park runs along the western edge with lakes, rivers and canals, and the Grand Union Canal crosses the borough. Large parks and woods include Hillingdon Court Park, Cowley Recreation Ground and Harefield's countryside.",
    "Transport depends heavily on location. The Metropolitan and Piccadilly lines serve Uxbridge, Ruislip and Hillingdon; the Central line reaches West Ruislip; the Elizabeth line serves Hayes & Harlington, West Drayton and Heathrow; and Chiltern trains stop at West Ruislip. Journeys to central London often take forty-five minutes to an hour. For someone working at Heathrow, Brunel or in the Thames Valley, that distance hardly matters, and the reward is woodland, lakes and canal paths closer to home than almost anywhere else in London.",
  ],

  hounslow: [
    "Hounslow is a west London borough stretching from Chiswick, beside the Thames, to Feltham and the edge of Heathrow. It is extremely varied: Chiswick is one of west London's most affluent neighbourhoods, while Hounslow town centre, Feltham and Cranford are busier, more diverse and more affordable, with large South Asian communities.",
    "Heathrow lies just beyond the borough's western boundary, and flight paths pass over much of the borough, particularly Hounslow and Isleworth. The airport is a major employer, and many residents work there or in nearby logistics and hospitality businesses.",
    "The borough has a surprising amount of heritage and green space. Syon Park, with its grand house, faces Kew Gardens across the river; Osterley Park, owned by the National Trust, has a Georgian mansion and large parkland; Chiswick House and Hogarth's House are in Chiswick; and Gunnersbury Park lies on the border with Ealing. The Great West Road is lined with art deco factories from the 1930s, and Brentford, beside the Thames, has become home to Brentford FC's stadium and new riverside housing.",
    "The Piccadilly line runs through Hounslow and Osterley towards Heathrow, the District line serves Chiswick, and South Western Railway trains serve Brentford, Isleworth, Hounslow and Feltham. Journeys to central London are generally reasonable from Chiswick and Brentford but longer from the western suburbs. Rents fall sharply from east to west: Chiswick is among the dearer places in west London, while Hounslow and Feltham offer some of the lowest rents on this side of the city, especially attractive to anyone working at the airport.",
  ],

  islington: [
    "Islington is one of the smallest boroughs in London by area and the most densely populated local authority in England. It runs from Clerkenwell and Finsbury, on the edge of the City, through Angel, Barnsbury, Canonbury and Highbury to Holloway, Archway and the lower slopes of Highgate.",
    "Upper Street is the borough's best-known high street, lined with restaurants, bars, theatres and shops, and Exmouth Market in Clerkenwell is known for its restaurants and street food. The borough has some of London's finest Georgian and early Victorian squares and terraces, alongside large council estates, and it has long been a place where wealthy residents and social housing tenants live side by side.",
    "Arsenal's Emirates Stadium, in Highbury, brings large crowds on match days. The borough has very little large green space, and among London boroughs it has one of the lowest amounts of park per resident, which makes Highbury Fields, Caledonian Park, Gillespie Park and the Regent's Canal especially valuable. Finsbury Park lies just beyond its northern boundary.",
    "Transport is excellent. The Northern line serves Angel and Archway, the Victoria line serves Highbury & Islington, the Piccadilly line serves Caledonian Road, Holloway Road and Arsenal, and the Overground and Great Northern services run through Highbury & Islington. The City is walkable or a short cycle from the southern parts of the borough. Most people who live here barely use a car and rarely take the tube for short trips, which is the real payoff for small flats, high rents and a shortage of parks.",
  ],

  "kensington-and-chelsea": [
    "The Royal Borough of Kensington and Chelsea is the smallest of London's thirty-two boroughs by area and one of the wealthiest places in the world. It includes Kensington, Chelsea, Knightsbridge, South Kensington, Notting Hill, Holland Park and Earl's Court, and it is home to Kensington Palace, the Natural History Museum, the V&A, the Science Museum, the Royal Albert Hall and Harrods.",
    "It is also a borough of extreme inequality. North Kensington, around Ladbroke Grove, Golborne Road and the Westway, has large social housing estates and long-established Caribbean, Moroccan, Portuguese and other communities, a short walk from some of the most expensive homes in Britain. The Grenfell Tower fire in 2017, which killed seventy-two people in North Kensington, brought those divisions into national focus.",
    "The borough's cultural life includes Portobello Road Market, the Notting Hill Carnival, the King's Road and Sloane Square's theatres and shops. Its green spaces include Holland Park, Kensington Gardens, which it borders, and the riverside at Chelsea Embankment, while Earl's Court is being transformed by a major redevelopment on the site of its former exhibition centre.",
    "Transport is generally good in Kensington, Notting Hill and South Kensington, with the Central, Circle, District and Piccadilly lines, but parts of Chelsea and North Kensington are further from stations. Its council tax is one of the lowest in London, but that saving is small beside rents that are among the highest anywhere in Britain, and the rental market is weighted towards furnished flats let to relocating professionals and international households.",
  ],

  "kingston-upon-thames": [
    "The Royal Borough of Kingston upon Thames is a south-west London borough of riverside towns, suburbs and green spaces. Kingston itself is a historic market town and large shopping centre, Surbiton is a classic Victorian and Edwardian commuter suburb, New Malden has one of the largest Korean communities in Europe, and Tolworth and Chessington stretch towards the Surrey border.",
    "The Thames runs along the borough's western edge, with riverside walks, rowing clubs and views across to Hampton Court Park and Bushy Park. Richmond Park lies just to the north, and the borough also includes Canbury Gardens, Fairfield Recreation Ground and the Hogsmill River. Chessington World of Adventures, a theme park and zoo, is in the borough's south.",
    "There is no Underground in Kingston. South Western Railway trains serve Kingston, Norbiton, Surbiton, New Malden and Berrylands, with the fastest services from Surbiton reaching Waterloo in around twenty minutes. Journeys from Kingston station, via Wimbledon or Richmond, take longer, and buses connect the borough to Wimbledon, Richmond and Heathrow.",
    "Kingston University brings a large student population, and the borough's schools, including Tiffin grammar schools, are a major draw for families. Council tax in Kingston is the highest of any London borough, which adds noticeably to monthly costs. The town centre is lively in the evenings by outer-London standards, thanks partly to the students, and for families the riverside, Richmond Park and the schools often outweigh both the slower trains from Kingston itself and the council tax bill.",
  ],

  lambeth: [
    "Lambeth is a long, narrow borough running south from the Thames at the South Bank to Streatham and West Norwood, and its character changes completely along the way. At its northern end are the London Eye, the Southbank Centre, the National Theatre, St Thomas' Hospital and Lambeth Palace, the London residence of the Archbishop of Canterbury. Further south are Vauxhall, Kennington, Stockwell, Brixton, Clapham, Herne Hill, Streatham and West Norwood.",
    "The borough is young, diverse and culturally influential. Brixton is at the heart of London's Caribbean community and its nightlife, food and music scenes, while Clapham is one of the city's biggest centres for young professionals. Stockwell and Vauxhall have large Portuguese-speaking communities, and Kennington and Herne Hill combine period housing with a quieter pace.",
    "Green spaces include Clapham Common, Brockwell Park, Kennington Park, Streatham Common and Norwood Park, and the borough has several lidos, including Brockwell Lido. The Oval cricket ground is in Kennington, and West Norwood Cemetery is one of London's grand Victorian cemeteries.",
    "Transport is strongest in the north and centre. The Victoria line serves Vauxhall, Stockwell and Brixton, and the Northern line serves Waterloo, Kennington, Oval, Stockwell and Clapham, but southern parts of the borough, including Streatham, Herne Hill and West Norwood, rely on rail services and buses. The difference between a flat on a main road in Brixton or Clapham and one ten minutes away beside Brockwell Park or in a Kennington square is enormous, so it pays to visit at night and at weekends before deciding.",
  ],

  lewisham: [
    "Lewisham is a south-east London borough of hills, rail lines and neighbourhoods with distinct identities. It includes Deptford and New Cross beside the river, Brockley and Ladywell's Victorian streets, Lewisham and Catford town centres, Forest Hill and Sydenham on the southern ridges, and part of Blackheath.",
    "The borough has a strong creative and community culture. Goldsmiths, University of London, in New Cross, has influenced art and music across the city, and Lewisham was London Borough of Culture in 2022. Its population is highly diverse, with large Caribbean, African and other communities, and many neighbourhoods have independent markets, cafés, pubs and venues.",
    "Green space is an important part of the borough's appeal. Hilly Fields, Blythe Hill Fields, Mountsfield Park and One Tree Hill offer views across London, while Ladywell Fields follows the River Ravensbourne. The Horniman Museum and Gardens are in Forest Hill, and Sydenham Hill Wood, part of the ancient Great North Wood, lies on the borough's western edge.",
    "There is no Underground in Lewisham. Southeastern and Southern trains serve many stations, reaching London Bridge in around ten to twenty minutes; the DLR runs from Lewisham to Canary Wharf and Bank; and the Overground runs through New Cross, Brockley and Forest Hill. Rents are generally below those in neighbouring Southwark, and the hilltop parks give many streets long views across London, which is a large part of why so many people who move to Brockley or Forest Hill for a year end up staying for ten.",
  ],

  merton: [
    "Merton is a south-west London borough best known for Wimbledon, but it also includes Mitcham, Morden, Colliers Wood, Raynes Park and South Wimbledon. Wimbledon, in the west, is affluent, green and famous for its tennis championships, while Mitcham and Morden, in the east, are more affordable, diverse and suburban.",
    "The River Wandle runs through the borough, and its riverside path connects Colliers Wood, Merton Abbey Mills and Morden Hall Park. Merton Abbey Mills, where William Morris once ran textile workshops, now has a market, shops and restaurants. Morden Hall Park, owned by the National Trust, has meadows, wetlands and historic buildings. Mitcham Common and Wimbledon Common provide large expanses of open land.",
    "The Northern line terminates at Morden and serves South Wimbledon and Colliers Wood, while the District line and South Western Railway serve Wimbledon, and Thameslink loops through Wimbledon, Morden South and South Merton. Tramlink connects Wimbledon and Mitcham to Croydon. Journeys to Waterloo from Wimbledon are fast, but eastern parts of the borough are more reliant on the Northern line and buses.",
    "Mitcham has one of the oldest cricket greens in the world, and AFC Wimbledon's stadium is on Plough Lane. Rents in Mitcham and Morden are well below Wimbledon's, often for more space, and the Northern line from Morden starts empty, so a seat into the City is almost guaranteed. Colliers Wood, between the two, has become a popular compromise, with a tube station, riverside walks along the Wandle and new flats near the station.",
  ],

  newham: [
    "Newham is one of the youngest and most ethnically diverse boroughs in Britain, and it has changed faster than almost anywhere in London since the 2012 Olympic Games. It includes Stratford and the Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park, the Royal Docks, Canning Town, West Ham, Forest Gate, East Ham and Upton Park.",
    "The Olympic legacy transformed Stratford into a major centre for shopping, transport and new homes. The Royal Docks, in the south, has ExCeL London, London City Airport, the cable car across the Thames and the Crystal, which now houses London's City Hall. East Ham's Green Street and Queen's Market are known for South Asian food, fabrics and shops, and West Ham United now plays at the London Stadium in the Olympic Park.",
    "The borough has significant deprivation alongside rapid regeneration, and many residents live in overcrowded or temporary housing, even as large new developments have been built. Its green spaces include the Olympic Park, West Ham Park, Wanstead Flats, Central Park in East Ham and the riverside at the Royal Docks.",
    "Transport is extensive. Stratford is one of London's best-connected stations, the Elizabeth line serves Stratford, Maryland, Forest Gate, Manor Park and Custom House, the Jubilee line reaches Stratford, West Ham and Canning Town, and the DLR serves the Royal Docks and Beckton. The District and Hammersmith & City lines serve Upton Park and East Ham. Renters here usually get a newer flat and a faster commute for their money than in Hackney or Tower Hamlets, but should expect building sites, changing streets and neighbourhoods that look different every year.",
  ],

  redbridge: [
    "Redbridge is an outer north-east London borough that ranges from busy town centres to leafy suburbs on the edge of Epping Forest. Ilford is its largest town and main shopping centre, while Wanstead, Woodford, South Woodford, Gants Hill, Barkingside and Hainault offer a mix of Victorian, interwar and post-war suburbs.",
    "The borough has large South Asian, Jewish and Eastern European communities, and Ilford Lane and the surrounding streets are known for South Asian restaurants and shops. Wanstead, in the west, has a village-like high street, a large green and Wanstead Park's lakes and woodland, and Woodford borders Epping Forest's ancient woodland. Redbridge's grammar schools are highly sought after.",
    "Green spaces include Valentines Park, Wanstead Flats on the borough's edge, Fairlop Waters, Hainault Forest Country Park and Epping Forest. The River Roding runs through the borough, with walking and cycling routes along parts of it.",
    "The Central line serves Wanstead, Redbridge, Gants Hill, Barkingside and Hainault on its loop, as well as Snaresbrook, South Woodford and Woodford. The Elizabeth line serves Ilford, Seven Kings, Goodmayes and Chadwell Heath with fast trains to Liverpool Street and central London. The choice within the borough is usually between two kinds of place: leafy Central line villages such as Wanstead and Woodford, which cost more, and busier Elizabeth line towns such as Ilford and Seven Kings, which are cheaper and faster to the City. Barkingside and Hainault, on the Central line loop, are quieter and more suburban again.",
  ],

  "richmond-upon-thames": [
    "Richmond upon Thames is the only London borough on both banks of the Thames, and it has more parkland and river frontage than any other part of the city. It includes Richmond, Twickenham, Barnes, Kew, Teddington, Hampton, East Sheen, Ham and Petersham, each with its own village or town centre.",
    "The borough's green spaces are exceptional. Richmond Park, Bushy Park, Kew Gardens, Hampton Court Park, Old Deer Park and Barnes Common all lie within or on its boundaries. Hampton Court Palace, Ham House, Marble Hill House and Strawberry Hill House are major historic houses, and Twickenham Stadium is the home of English rugby.",
    "The borough is affluent, family-oriented and known for strong schools, and rents and property prices are among the highest in outer London. Aircraft approaching Heathrow pass over much of the borough, particularly Richmond, Kew and Barnes, which is worth checking when choosing a street.",
    "Transport is mainly rail-based. The District line and Overground terminate at Richmond, South Western Railway trains serve most of the borough's towns, and Kew Gardens has both District line and Overground services. There is no Underground in Twickenham, Teddington or Barnes, and journeys to east London are long. Council tax sits towards the top of the London range, and rents match the setting. People pay it for the river, the royal parks and villages such as Teddington and Kew that feel far removed from the city while remaining a train ride from Waterloo.",
  ],

  southwark: [
    "Southwark runs from the Thames at Bankside and London Bridge south to Dulwich and the edge of Crystal Palace, and it contains some of London's most varied neighbourhoods. Its northern stretch includes Tate Modern, Shakespeare's Globe, Borough Market, the Shard and Guy's Hospital. Further south are Bermondsey, Rotherhithe, Elephant and Castle, Walworth, Camberwell, Peckham, East Dulwich and Dulwich Village.",
    "The borough has one of the largest council housing stocks in England, and many neighbourhoods combine social housing with period terraces and new developments. Regeneration has transformed Elephant and Castle, Canada Water and the riverside, while Peckham and Camberwell have become known for art, food and nightlife. Dulwich Village, in the south, is quieter and more affluent, home to Dulwich College and Dulwich Picture Gallery, the world's first purpose-built public art gallery.",
    "Green spaces include Burgess Park, Southwark Park, Peckham Rye, Dulwich Park, Belair Park and Sydenham Hill Wood, and the Thames Path runs along the borough's northern edge. The population is young and diverse, with large West African, Caribbean and Latin American communities.",
    "Transport is strongest in the north. The Jubilee line serves Southwark, London Bridge, Bermondsey and Canada Water, the Northern line serves Borough, London Bridge and Elephant and Castle, and the Bakerloo line ends at Elephant and Castle. Southern parts of the borough rely on Overground, Southern, Southeastern and Thameslink services and buses. The borough's range of prices is wider than almost anywhere else in London: a room in a Peckham house share and a riverside flat beside Tate Modern can be two miles and thousands of pounds a month apart.",
  ],

  sutton: [
    "Sutton is an outer south London borough on the Surrey border, made up of suburbs and small towns including Sutton, Cheam, Carshalton, Wallington, Belmont and Worcester Park. It is known for its quiet residential streets, relatively low crime and highly regarded schools, including several grammar schools.",
    "Carshalton has one of the borough's most attractive centres, with ponds, historic houses and Honeywood Museum, while Cheam has a village feel and the remains of Henry VIII's Nonsuch Palace in Nonsuch Park. Hackbridge is home to BedZED, one of the first large eco-villages in Britain, and Belmont has the Royal Marsden Hospital and Institute of Cancer Research.",
    "Green spaces include Nonsuch Park, Oaks Park, Beddington Park, the River Wandle and the Wandle Valley Regional Park. The borough's population is more settled than inner London's, and family houses with gardens make up much of the housing stock.",
    "There is no Underground in Sutton. Southern trains serve Sutton, Carshalton, Wallington, Cheam and Belmont, with services to Victoria and London Bridge, and Thameslink trains loop through Sutton, Carshalton and Hackbridge towards Wimbledon, Blackfriars and St Pancras. Tramlink touches the borough at Beddington Lane. Journeys to central London often take thirty to forty minutes. Few people move to Sutton for excitement; they move for schools, gardens and quiet, and the borough's grammar schools in particular draw families from well beyond its boundaries. Worcester Park and Cheam tend to cost more, while Wallington and parts of Sutton town centre offer more flats and lower rents.",
  ],

  "tower-hamlets": [
    "Tower Hamlets is one of the most densely populated and fastest-changing boroughs in London. It stretches from the Tower of London and the City fringe through Whitechapel, Spitalfields, Bethnal Green, Bow and Mile End to Limehouse, Poplar, Canary Wharf and the Isle of Dogs.",
    "It is home to Britain's largest Bangladeshi community, centred around Brick Lane, Whitechapel and Shadwell, and has a young and diverse population. The borough contains some of London's richest and poorest neighbourhoods: Canary Wharf's financial towers and luxury flats stand close to areas with high child poverty and overcrowding.",
    "Its cultural and historic sites include the Tower of London, Whitechapel Gallery, Columbia Road Flower Market, Spitalfields Market, Brick Lane, the Young V&A and the riverside at Wapping and Limehouse. Victoria Park, Mile End Park, Tower Hamlets Cemetery Park and the Regent's Canal provide green space, though much of the borough is dense and built up.",
    "Transport is excellent. The Elizabeth line serves Whitechapel and Canary Wharf, the Jubilee line serves Canary Wharf, the Central line serves Bethnal Green and Mile End, the District and Hammersmith & City lines run through the borough, and the DLR and Overground cover much of the rest. Within a single borough, a renter can choose between a warehouse flat in Wapping, a new tower beside Canary Wharf, a Victorian terrace near Victoria Park and a room above a shop off Brick Lane, all within a short ride of the City.",
  ],

  "waltham-forest": [
    "Waltham Forest is a north-east London borough stretching from Leyton and Leytonstone in the south to Walthamstow and Chingford in the north. It was London's first Borough of Culture, in 2019, and it has become known for independent businesses, community projects and one of the most ambitious low-traffic street schemes in the country.",
    "Walthamstow is the borough's largest town, with its long street market, Walthamstow Village conservation area and the William Morris Gallery in the house where Morris grew up. Leyton and Leytonstone, further south, are near the Olympic Park and have growing café and pub scenes; Leytonstone is also the birthplace of Alfred Hitchcock. Chingford, in the north, borders Epping Forest and has Queen Elizabeth's Hunting Lodge, a Tudor timber-framed building.",
    "Green space is a major attraction. Epping Forest runs along the borough's eastern edge, Walthamstow Wetlands and Walthamstow Marshes lie in the Lee Valley to the west, and Lloyd Park, Leyton Jubilee Park and Hollow Pond provide more open space. The borough's Mini Holland scheme has created segregated cycle lanes and quieter residential streets.",
    "The Victoria line serves Walthamstow Central and Blackhorse Road, the Central line serves Leyton and Leytonstone, and the Overground runs from Chingford and Walthamstow to Liverpool Street as well as across north London. Rents have risen quickly since the borough became fashionable, but most of it still costs less than Hackney next door, and the forest, the wetlands and the quieter streets are what keep people here once they arrive.",
  ],

  wandsworth: [
    "Wandsworth is a south-west London borough of commons, riverside and popular residential neighbourhoods, including Battersea, Putney, Wandsworth Town, Tooting, Balham, Earlsfield, Southfields and Roehampton. It is especially popular with young professionals and families, and its council tax has for many years been the lowest of any London borough.",
    "The Thames runs along its northern edge, and the borough has some of the best open spaces in London: Battersea Park, Wandsworth Common, Tooting Common, Putney Heath, part of Wimbledon Common and part of Clapham Common. The regenerated Battersea Power Station and Nine Elms area has brought new shops, offices and thousands of homes, as well as the US Embassy.",
    "Housing ranges from Victorian terraces and mansion blocks to large post-war estates, including the Alton Estate in Roehampton, and new riverside towers. Streets between the commons, sometimes nicknamed Nappy Valley for their many young families, are among the most sought-after family neighbourhoods in south London.",
    "Transport is strong. Clapham Junction is one of the busiest rail stations in Britain, with trains to Waterloo, Victoria and across south London; the Northern line serves Tooting, Balham and Battersea Power Station; the District line serves Putney and Southfields; and South Western Railway serves Putney, Wandsworth Town and Earlsfield. The low council tax is a genuine saving, but it is modest compared with the rents, which reflect how many people want to live between these commons. Southfields and Roehampton, on the borough's western side, are often cheaper than Putney or Wandsworth Town while still close to Wimbledon Common and Richmond Park.",
  ],

  westminster: [
    "The City of Westminster is the political, royal and commercial heart of London. It contains the Houses of Parliament, Buckingham Palace, Downing Street, Trafalgar Square, the West End's theatres, Oxford Street, Soho, Mayfair and Covent Garden, as well as residential neighbourhoods such as Pimlico, Marylebone, Bayswater, Maida Vale and St John's Wood.",
    "Its residential character varies enormously. Mayfair, Belgravia and St John's Wood are among the most expensive places in the world, while Church Street, Queen's Park and parts of Westbourne and Harrow Road have large social housing estates and significant deprivation. Soho and Covent Garden have active resident communities that live among theatres, bars and restaurants.",
    "Westminster has extraordinary green space for a central borough. Hyde Park, Green Park, St James's Park and much of Regent's Park lie within or on its boundaries, alongside Paddington Recreation Ground and the canals of Little Venice. The borough's council tax is among the lowest in London, which helps offset high rents.",
    "Transport is unmatched. Almost every Underground line passes through Westminster, and major rail terminals at Victoria, Paddington, Marylebone and Charing Cross serve the borough, while the Elizabeth line stops at Paddington, Bond Street and Tottenham Court Road. Many residents walk or cycle to work. For people who work in Westminster, the West End or the City, the time saved can be worth more than the rent premium, although the flats that come with it are usually small for the price.",
  ],
};
